---
import DefaultLayout from '../../layouts/DefaultLayout.astro'
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro'
import MethodologyNav from '../../components/MethodologyNav.astro'
import { asset } from '../../lib/asset'

const K = 'page.methodology.measurements.'

const predicates = [
  { term: 'a', iri: 'isq:MeasuredQuantity', def: 'The measurement result — Qm = {Q}·[Q]; subclass of isq:QuantityInstantiation.' },
  { term: 'isq:measurementOf', iri: '→ isq:QuantityInstantiation', def: 'The particular quantity that was measured (required, exactly one).' },
  { term: 'isq:hasValue', iri: 'xsd:decimal', def: 'The numerical value {Q} (required, exactly one).' },
  { term: 'isq:hasUnit', iri: '→ isq:Unit', def: 'The unit [Q] (required, exactly one).' },
  { term: 'isq:uncertainty', iri: 'xsd:decimal', def: 'Expanded uncertainty U, per GUM (optional).' },
  { term: 'isq:measuredAt', iri: 'xsd:dateTime', def: 'When the measurement was performed (optional).' },
]
---

<DefaultLayout titleKey="page.methodology.browser_title" description="The ISQ Browser measurement intake contract: supply your own measurement records as RDF using the Pendrill model — the browser provides the vocabulary and rendering, never the data.">
  <div>
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-gradient-to-br from-cyan-950 via-slate-950 to-brand-950">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 hero-pattern"></div>
      <div class="grain-overlay absolute inset-0"></div>
      <div class="relative z-10 max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
        <div class="max-w-3xl page-enter">
          <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Methodology', href: '/methodology' }, { label: 'Measurements' }]} variant="dark" />
          <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight heading-serif leading-tight" data-i18n={K + 'title'}>Bring your own measurements</h1>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-300/80 leading-relaxed" data-i18n={K + 'intro'}>
            The ISQ Browser is a standards browser — it publishes the ISO 80000 &amp; IEC 80000 vocabulary and the measurement model, never measurement data itself.
            Organisations that hold measurements supply them through a simple RDF intake contract, and this site renders them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="absolute bottom-0 inset-x-0 h-16 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-50 dark:from-dark-950 to-transparent z-10"></div>
    </section>

    <MethodologyNav active="measurements" />

    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 space-y-12">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 heading-serif mb-2" data-i18n={K + 'contract_title'}>The contract</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 mb-5" data-i18n={K + 'contract_intro'}>
          A measurement record is a small RDF graph using the ISQ ontology terms. One file, no API, no schema of our invention — the ontology <em>is</em> the format.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-separate border-spacing-y-1">
            <thead>
              <tr class="text-left text-xs text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider">
                <th class="px-3 py-1.5" data-i18n={K + 'th_predicate'}>Predicate</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-1.5" data-i18n={K + 'th_object'}>Object</th>
                <th class="px-3 py-1.5" data-i18n={K + 'th_meaning'}>Meaning</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {predicates.map((p) => (
                <tr class="bg-white dark:bg-dark-800">
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 border border-r-0 border-slate-200/60 dark:border-dark-600/60 rounded-l-lg whitespace-nowrap">{p.term}</td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 border-y border-slate-200/60 dark:border-dark-600/60 whitespace-nowrap">{p.iri}</td>
                  <td class="px-3 py-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 border border-l-0 border-slate-200/60 dark:border-dark-600/60 rounded-r-lg">{p.def}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 heading-serif mb-2" data-i18n={K + 'intake_title'}>The intake path</h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
          <li data-i18n-html={K + 'intake_1'}>Provide a Turtle file using the <code class="text-xs font-mono">isq:</code> terms above — see the <a href={asset('/ontologies/isq-examples.ttl')} class="text-brand-600 dark:text-brand-400 font-medium hover:underline">worked example</a> for a complete graph (rod, length of rod, instantiation, measured result with uncertainty).</li>
          <li data-i18n={K + 'intake_2'}>Extend <code class="text-xs font-mono">browser/public/ontologies/</code> with the file (a downstream deployment can add it at build time; no code changes are needed).</li>
          <li data-i18n-html={K + 'intake_3'}>The ontology plugin extracts every new individual automatically: it gets a detail page with its Assertions and Values, RDF exports, and appears in <a href={asset('/ontology/measurements')} class="text-brand-600 dark:text-brand-400 font-medium hover:underline">the measurements index</a>.</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="mt-5 p-4 rounded-xl bg-amber-50/50 dark:bg-amber-950/20 border border-amber-200/50 dark:border-amber-800/30 text-xs text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl" data-i18n={K + 'governance_note'}>
          Note: the browser does not validate measurement quality or provenance — that remains the data provider's responsibility (instrument, procedure, and operator metadata can be attached using the same RDF approach, e.g. <code class="font-mono">prov:</code> or <code class="font-mono">smart:Activity</code>).
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100 heading-serif mb-2" data-i18n={K + 'why_title'}>Why a contract, not content</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl" data-i18n={K + 'why_body'}>
          Measurement data is inherently third-party: it belongs to laboratories, registries, and industry. Bundling measurements here would blur the line between the standard and its application — precisely the boundary Pendrill (2024) draws at level 3. The contract keeps that boundary clean: the standard defines the quantities, the ontology defines the measurement structure, and whoever measures owns the results.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</DefaultLayout>
